<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Bakwena Chiefdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1700 CE – 1885 CE (approx.) · capital Molepolole</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Tswana chiefdom of the Kwena, whose ruler Sechele I was David Livingstone's only convert and a shrewd defender of his people.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakwena">Bakwena</a> trace their origin to a split from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahurutshe">Bahurutshe</a> and later gave rise to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamangwato">Bangwato</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangwaketse">Bangwaketse</a> through further divisions. By the 18th century the Kwena were settled in what is now south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a>, though they were scattered during the wars of the 1820s.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sechele-i">Sechele I</a> rebuilt the chiefdom from the 1830s, ruling through the kgotla and ward system. He welcomed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-livingstone">David Livingstone</a>, was baptised in 1848, and kept trade routes open to the north. In 1852 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/south-african-republic">Transvaal</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> attacked his town at Dimawe, but Sechele survived and made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakwena">Kwena</a> a regional power, settling at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molepolole">Molepolole</a> in the 1860s.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Fearing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> expansion, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tswana-people">Tswana</a> chiefs accepted British protection, and in 1885 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kweneng-district">Kweneng</a> became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bechuanaland-protectorate">Bechuanaland Protectorate</a>. The chiefdom continued under colonial rule and is part of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1700 CE – 1885 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:93.53%;width:3.68%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1700 CE – 1885 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Molepolole</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sechele I, Sebele I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional Tswana religion, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Setswana</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/botswana">Botswana</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kwena</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1792&amp;focus=bakwena-chiefdom" aria-label="Bakwena Chiefdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5358 3076 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bakwena Chiefdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5678 3332L5688 3341"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5688 3341h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5678 3332h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5678 3332h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Molepolole. <a href="/atlas?year=1792&amp;focus=bakwena-chiefdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

